1. THE AXIOM ON V_4
   ∀v0 ∀v1 (∀v2 (v2 ∈ v0 ⇔ v2 ∈ v1) ⇒ v0 ≃ v1)
   for every a and b: if every set is in a exactly when it is in b,
   then a and b are the same set.
   16 sets, 256 pairs (a, b) checked: True

2. HOW IT IS USED: TO PROVE a = b, PROVE a ⊆ b AND b ⊆ a
   a = {1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12}, written out;  b = {d : 1 ≤ d ≤ 12 and d divides 12}
   every member of a is in b: True
   every member of b is in a: True
   so a = b: True   (Python's == on sets is the axiom)
   Order and repetition are not members, so they do not count:
   {2, 5} == {5, 2}: True   {1, 1, 1} == {1}: True

3. 'UNIQUE BY EXTENSIONALITY': EACH AXIOM'S SET HAS EXACTLY ONE CANDIDATE
   for every a, b in V_3, the number of sets in V_4 whose members are
   exactly a and b: [1].  The pair exists (the axiom of pairs),
   and extensionality says two candidates would have to be equal,
   so 'the pair {a, b}' names one set. The same sentence follows
   every other axiom in the book.

4. A UNIVERSE WHERE IT FAILS: TWO POINTS WITH NO MEMBERS
   points a, b, c with a, b memberless and c = {a}
   extensionality: False
   witness: a and b have the same members (True), and a ≃ b is False
   Such points are called atoms or urelements. ZF has none: by
   extensionality there is exactly one memberless set, ∅, and every
   other set is built from it.
